            <ArticleTitle>Cardiorespiratory Changes and Pain Response of Lift Laparoscopy Compared to Capnoperitoneum Laparoscopy in Dogs.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>7-14</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1532-950X.2014.12198.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To compare intraoperative physiologic variables and post-operative pain associated with lift laparoscopy and conventional capnoperitoneum laparoscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective randomized case controlled study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="ANIMALS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Healthy dogs (n = 30).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Dogs having laparoscopic ovariohysterectomy were randomly assigned to lift laparoscopy (n = 14) or capnoperitoneum (16) laparoscopy. Physiologic variables measured intraoperatively were documented. Postoperatively, pain response was assessed in a blinded fashion using the short Glasgow pain scale and von Frey filament aesthesiometry.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Lift laparoscopy was associated with less frequency of hypercapnia, required less anesthetic gas, and was not more time-consuming or painful than capnoperitoneum laparoscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Lift laparoscopy is a feasible alternative to capnoperitoneum laparoscopy, especially in dogs where pressurized capnoperitoneum is not desired.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© Copyright 2014 by The American College of Veterinary Surgeons.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
